HashWires Java port notice
==========================

Upstream source: https://github.com/novifinancial/hashwires
Recorded upstream snapshot: 473f726f243c8ac1329d9eccda0947f0a111311e
Upstream license: MIT (see LICENSE in this directory).

This directory contains an independent Java 17 port of the optimized ordinary
(one-time) HashWires implementation. The port follows the protocol structure in
upstream src/dp.rs, src/hashes.rs, src/hashwires.rs, src/serialization.rs, and
src/shuffle.rs:

* minimum dominating partitions;
* shared hash multichains;
* padded linear accumulation;
* 16-byte per-MDP salts;
* deterministic Durstenfeld-style leaf placement; and
* a deterministic padded sparse Merkle accumulator.

The port uses the JDK SHA-256 MessageDigest provider and a domain-separated
SHA-256 counter sampler for deterministic shuffling. Its canonical Java proof
encoding is designed for the paper's element-count accounting and is not
claimed to be byte-for-byte interoperable with the Rust crate serialization.

For the MDP boundary loop, the port follows paper Algorithm 1 literally:
b^i is processed whenever b^i <= x, because d = ceil(log_b(x + 1)). This fixes
the exact-power edge case caused by the strict `b^i < x` loop in the recorded
Rust snapshot. Branch selection checks digit-wise dominance directly rather
than relying only on numeric ordering. To match this artifact's [0, 2^bits)
input domain, zero is represented by one significant zero digit.
The ordinary construction's internal sparse Merkle accumulator is included.
The Section 5.2 outer T-time Merkle wrapper and optional checksum chain are not
implemented. External issuer authentication of the final 32-byte commitment is
an application-layer operation and is not included in the protocol timings.
